mmc: host: rda: report a missing response as a timeout

SDMMC_STATUS_NO_RSP_ERR means the card never answered the command, but
rda_mmc_recv_resp() returned without setting cmd->error. The core then
treats the command as successful and reads cmd->resp[], which is still
zero.

For SDIO that produces a badly misleading diagnosis. mmc_attach_sdio()
sends CMD5 (IO_SEND_OP_COND) and uses resp[0] as the card's OCR; a zero
OCR makes mmc_select_voltage() print

    mmc1: no support for card's volts
    mmc1: error -22 whilst initialising SDIO card

which reads as a voltage-range mismatch when in fact nothing responded at
all. The host's ocr_avail already matches the vendor's SDIO host exactly
(MMC_VDD_32_33 | MMC_VDD_33_34, devices.c rda_mmc1_data), so the voltage
was never the problem.

Set -ETIMEDOUT when a response was expected and none arrived.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [local forward-port pending proper upstream submission]

Signed-off-by: Sergio Marin <sergio.marin@pantacor.com>
---

diff --git a/drivers/mmc/host/rda-mmc.c b/drivers/mmc/host/rda-mmc.c
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/rda-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/rda-mmc.c
@@ -175,8 +175,19 @@
 		return;
 	}
 
-	if (status & SDMMC_STATUS_NO_RSP_ERR)
+	if (status & SDMMC_STATUS_NO_RSP_ERR) {
+		/*
+		 * No response at all. Returning success here leaves cmd->resp[]
+		 * zeroed and the core reads that as a valid answer -- for CMD5
+		 * (SDIO IO_SEND_OP_COND) a zero OCR comes back out of
+		 * mmc_select_voltage() as the thoroughly misleading
+		 * "no support for card's volts" instead of "the card never
+		 * answered". A missing response is a timeout; say so.
+		 */
+		if (mmc_resp_type(cmd) != MMC_RSP_NONE)
+			cmd->error = -ETIMEDOUT;
 		return;
+	}
 
 	if (status & SDMMC_STATUS_RSP_ERR) {
 		cmd->error = -EILSEQ;
